====================================================================================================
TELEMETRY UP-LINK — firmware emitters, app parser, and the unit the model is told
====================================================================================================

-- 1. which telemetry frames can this board emit? (OPENBOT = DIY_ESP32) --------------
frame            producer                   flag that gates it                 state on this board
v<volts>         openbot.ino:1759-1761      HAS_VOLTAGE_DIVIDER = 0            COMPILED OUT
s<cm>            openbot.ino:1865-1867      HAS_SONAR = 0                      COMPILED OUT
w<rpm>,<rpm>     openbot.ino:1777-1783      HAS_SPEED_SENSORS_FRONT = 0        COMPILED OUT
b<id>            openbot.ino:1457-1459      HAS_BUMPER = None                  COMPILED OUT
r                setup(), openbot.ino:942   -                                  compiled in

-> the only frame the app can ever receive from this board is the boot banner 'r'.

-- 2. the app's parser, on a frame per sensor -----------------------------------------
s25                    -> 'obstacle 25 cm'         'sonar at STOP_DISTANCE (10 cm is the firmware cutoff) -- 25 cm here'
s0                     -> 'obstacle 0 cm'          'sonar floored'
s300                   -> 'obstacle 300 cm'        'MAX_SONAR_DISTANCE'
v12.34                 -> 'battery 12.34 V'        'battery'
w210,196               -> 'wheels 210,196 rpm'     'wheel odometry'
bcf                    -> 'bump cf'                'bumper'
r                      -> 'robot ready'            'boot banner'
fDIY:v:s:              -> None                     'feature reply (never requested by the app)'
Control: 180,128       -> None                     "the DEBUG echo of a control frame -- first char 'C', not 'c'"
Heartbeat Interval: 500 -> None                     'the DEBUG echo of a heartbeat'

-- 3. what the model is told, for the same readings -----------------------------------
GroqClient.kt:89-96 ships SensorSnapshot.frontDistanceMm under the key 'frontDistanceMm'.
The firmware's sonar value is CENTIMETRES (openbot.ino:669-670 comment), so a naive
frontDistanceMm = <sonar value> is a 10x under-report of the true distance.

wire frame     cm         mm (!)           what the model concludes     ObstacleAvoidanceController verdict
s25            25         25               obstacle at 0.03 m, must stop BLOCKED (real 0.25 m)
s60            60         60               obstacle at 0.06 m, must stop BLOCKED (real 0.60 m)
s100           100        100              obstacle at 0.10 m, must stop CAUTION (real 1.00 m)
s150           150        150              obstacle at 0.15 m, must stop clear (real 1.50 m)
s300           300        300              obstacle at 0.30 m, must stop clear (real 3.00 m)

-- 4. what the model is told: who supplies SensorSnapshot ----------------------------
  MainActivity passes sensorProvider = { sensorSnapshot }                yes
  OcrScreen publishes the scene the controller decided on (onSceneSnapshot) yes
  the metres -> millimetres conversion lives in one function             yes
  a robot that cannot see says so (hazardsKnown reaches the model)       yes

Consumer behaviour with a live scene: the same SceneAwarenessResult the safety controller
decides on is what the model is told, in millimetres, with hazardsKnown=false whenever
there is no depth frame - so a clear-path claim always has evidence behind it.
